---
// How I work: first person, on the floor, one clinic at a time. Capacity is
// stated as a plain fact, never as a pitch. Side cards: current status, who
// this fits, and who it does not.
---
<section id="how" class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head rv">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> How I work</p>
      <h2>Inside the clinic, one clinic at a time.</h2>
      <p class="lead">Large companies call this a forward-deployed engineer: one person who sits with the people doing the work, builds the fix, and stays until it holds. I do that for a clinic.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="hw">
      <ol class="hw-steps rv">
        <li>
          <div>
            <h3>I start on your floor, not in a meeting.</h3>
            <p>A week at the front desk, in the imaging room and in the exam lanes. I write down what people re-type, re-check, or walk to find out. That list is the plan.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div>
            <h3>I build the smallest thing that removes the problem.</h3>
            <p>Usually within weeks, not quarters. A board on a TV, a script on a device PC, a kiosk at the desk. Staff see it the week it is ready, and they tell me what is wrong with it.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div>
            <h3>It runs beside your EMR. Nothing is replaced.</h3>
            <p>Everything reads from and writes to the EMR you already have, through the same screens your staff log into. OSCAR stays OSCAR. Patient data stays on your own network.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div>
            <h3>I stay until staff stop noticing it.</h3>
            <p>The tools start themselves every morning, restart themselves when something hiccups, and send me a weekly health note. When something breaks, I hear about it before you do.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="hw-side rv">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Right now</h3>
          <div class="hw-status">
            <div class="row"><span>Deployed at</span><b>Tri-City Eye Care, Kitchener, Ontario</b></div>
            <div class="row"><span>Since</span><b>May 2026 (first board). Newest tool live October 2026.</b></div>
            <div class="row"><span>EMR</span><b>OSCAR. Other browser-based EMRs on request.</b></div>
            <div class="row"><span>Next start</span><b>One clinic, 2027</b></div>
            <div class="row"><span>By</span><b>Introduction, or a note from you below</b></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>A good fit</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>A clinic that sees 60 or more patients a day across several rooms and roles.</li>
            <li>A manager or owner who is tired of being the glue between systems.</li>
            <li>Diagnostic devices that print or export, and a daysheet that changes all day.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card quiet">
          <h3>Not a fit</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>A clinic looking to replace its EMR. I build beside yours, not instead of it.</li>
            <li>A one-doctor office where the front desk can already see every room.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
